window.docContents[137].resolve({"/Encapsulation/Classes-and-visibility/#Core-C______--Encapsulation--Classes-and-visibility":{"contents":"A class has an optional base, fields and methods with a visibility, at most one constructor named after the class and at most one destructor. Members before the first section label are private, as in C++ (N4659 §14 paragraph 3).The class is well formed, , when the conditions of ClassOk hold. The base exists and the chain of bases is acyclic. The fields have distinct names, repeat no field of a base, and have storable, non void and well formed types. No method has the name of a field. Two methods of one name are distinguishable, , and no signature occurs twice. An operator member takes one parameter, .A method that redefines a method of a base finds it virtual there, carries override and keeps the signature, and override marks only a redefinition. The result types are void or storable, the parameter types bindable, and all are well formed. The constructor of the base takes no parameters, since there are no initialiser lists. Every member body is well typed under this, and a member that returns a reference satisfies T-RetRef of .The context of a member body binds this to  and the parameters as variables, so the current class is the class of this in .\n\nA private member declared in class  is visible exactly when . Visibility is thus a typing rule. The public section of a class is the signature of an abstract data type, and the private section is its representation, reachable only from the member bodies of the class.The same premise applies to methods, .\n\nInside a member body this is the location of the receiver. The call binds it in  as an alias, never as a variable, and it evaluates to a pointer. An unqualified name that is not a variable denotes the member of this, both in the type checker and in the evaluator.The same holds of a call. An unqualified name that is neither a variable nor a function of the program is a member of the receiver. The evaluator also requires that no header declares the name. The annotation of  rewrites such a call into this->f(…), so CallThis applies only to a program that runs as parsed.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Encapsulation","header":"6.1. Classes and visibility","id":"/Encapsulation/Classes-and-visibility/#Core-C______--Encapsulation--Classes-and-visibility"}});